WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:01.580
. It is great to welcome back to the program,
Richard Wolffe, professor emeritus of economics

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:02.900
at my alma mater, the University of Massachusetts.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.200
He taught me introductory macroeconomics a
long, long time ago.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.150
Currently a visiting professor at New School
University in New York City.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.269
The new book is The Sickness Is the System
When Capitalism Fails to Save US from Pandemics

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:15.269
or itself.

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:22.240
And you make the point in the book that I've
been making for some time, which is that the

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.820
pandemic exposed the inherent fragility of
many systems which we take for granted.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.700
This includes the streams of income from our
jobs, whatever they may be.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.980
This includes the food supply chain, as we
very quickly saw in grocery stores, toilet

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.650
paper, the inability of government, our government.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.760
Others have moved far more quickly, but the
inability of our government to quickly react

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.210
to changing circumstances give us the kind
of big picture of what what the takeaway should

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.210
be.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.130
Well, I think it's a stark illustration of
something which has been difficult for many

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.920
Americans to get their their heads around.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.160
So let me be blunt and make the point that
the book makes in a variety of ways.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.370
If you think about it, we all know that we
had a terrible virus back in 1918, killed

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.370
600000 Americans.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.280
We know very well how damaging viruses are.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.899
In recent years, we have studied Meurs, Saar's,
Ebola.

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:11.039
I mean, there is no excuse for a modern country,
let alone a rich one, let alone an economy

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:14.829
with a well-developed medical practice and
education system to be unprepared for a virus.

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:16.179
There really is no excuse.

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:18.909
And when you remember that an economic system,
if it has any priority, the public health

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:22.149
of the people who live in it has to be right
up there.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:23.149
Number one, two or three.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.149
Why in the world were we unprepared?

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:27.759
We had companies that can produce masks and
gloves and ventilators and all of that, but

00:01:27.759 --> 00:01:28.939
they didn't do it.

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:32.770
And I know why I'm an economist when I study,
it isn't profitable.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.689
No company wants to produce masks by the millions.

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:37.139
Store them in warehouses around the country
where the population is concentrated.

00:01:37.139 --> 00:01:38.649
Make sure that they are clean.

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:40.159
Make sure that they don't deteriorate.

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:41.579
Replace those that do watch the whole thing.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:45.219
And then you never know when the next virus
will be.

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:51.119
Will you have to sit on all of this for two
years, for six years or for 20 years?

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:52.159
So it's not profitable.

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:54.569
It's not a rational capitalist, profit driven
calculation to make.

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:58.899
So the companies didn't and we didn't have
the ventilators, the gloves, the masks and

00:01:58.899 --> 00:01:59.899
all the rest.

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:01.899
And there is no shorthand way of saying it.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:04.159
Private capitalism and the public health don't
work well together.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:05.259
They don't solve the problem.

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:06.259
OK.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.450
We could have had a secondary solution.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.670
The government could have come in and said
what I just said, private capitalism is not

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.790
capable of making us prepared for a virus.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.790
So we're going to do it.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.220
We're going to buy the masks as fast as you
produce.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.250
Did the ventilators, did other gloves.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.980
And then we're going to store them at taxpayer
expense.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.140
In fact, in warehouses and distribute them
and monitor them and clean them and replace

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.140
them.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.329
We'll do all that because private capitalism
fails.

00:02:23.329 --> 00:02:25.739
The government is going to come in and compensate.

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:31.280
But we didn't do that either because our government
is trapped in this ideology that somehow private

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.280
capitalism does everything maximally efficiently.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.280
Maximov got it all wrong.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.280
Well, they did have they did have the national
stockpile.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.280
Right.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.280
I mean, in a sense, that's what it is.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.280
It was completely mis distributed.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.280
And they did nothing with it for months.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.280
But to some degree, isn't that what the national
stockpile is?

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.280
Yes.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.170
And look, we have plenty of examples of where
the government can do it.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.950
The best example that I use is defense right
now.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.870
It's not profitable to make a missile and
store it for 10 years or a gun or a ship or

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.870
a plane.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.870
But we do it and the government comes right
in, buys the thing as fast as the defense

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.900
companies produce it, and then the government
pays to store it.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.900
Monitor it and clean it.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.000
So we know how to do it.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.360
But it was done because we're so ideologically
block that what we can do for defense we won't

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.510
actually do for our own personal public health.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.290
We've been cutting back on rural hospitals.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.090
We've been cutting back on that stockpile.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.730
We have now shown ourselves to have what?

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.319
I mean, here's the statistic that I think
ends all conversation.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:19.280
The United States has four and a half percent
of the world's people and we have 25 percent

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.680
of the world's colvard cases and colvert deaths.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.680
We're a rich country.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.830
That is a statistic that screams something
fundamental is wrong in our society with our

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.069
capability has such a record.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:30.069
So on the economic response, which countries
did best in terms of dealing with the financial

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:31.170
insecurity, the job loss as a result of social
distancing measures that were, what are the

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.170
examples we should look to?

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.650
Yeah, I'm afraid that there again, I have
bad news, but it's bad news.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:36.879
It's also a wakeup call.

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:37.879
Yeah.

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:38.879
United States did not do well.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:39.879
Let me give you the starkest example.

00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:41.151
In the United States over the last six months,
the richest people in the country gained tens

00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:42.151
of billions more of their wealth.

00:03:42.151 --> 00:03:43.151
Those numbers are well known.

00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:44.151
I won't repeat them.

00:03:44.151 --> 00:03:45.151
Over the same period of time, more than 50
million people had to file for unemployment

00:03:45.151 --> 00:03:46.180
compensation either by the state or the federal
government.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.749
I mean, a starker rendition of inequality
you could not have.

00:03:48.749 --> 00:03:55.340
At the same time, we were all telling each
other, this virus is a great danger to all

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.340
of us.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.340
We all have to work hard.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.340
Together, we will get through this together.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.609
Yeah, some people got through it together
by becoming richer.

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:00.609
And an awful lot of people are getting through
it by becoming poorer because of unemployment.

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:01.639
Why do I stress this in the European countries?

00:04:01.639 --> 00:04:02.639
It wasn't done.

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:05.430
Unemployment in Italy during the entire Colden
period was less than it was a year before

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:10.719
that, when there was no cold in German, unemployment
went from five percent roughly in March to

00:04:10.719 --> 00:04:11.719
six percent roughly.

00:04:11.719 --> 00:04:17.170
Now, in short, in every major European country,
they did not do what the United States did.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:21.580
They didn't throw tens of millions of people
out of work, throwing them on unemployment,

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.170
adding to their losses of unemployment, the
unspeakable anxiety of wondering whether they'll

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.570
ever have a job again, whether the job they
lost would be there when they went back looking

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:28.840
for it, which we all know.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:29.840
Millions of them won't.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:30.840
We already know that.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.990
And so they're all wondering, is the roulette
wheel going to come down and point on me to

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.650
add anxiety to the worries about the illness,
to the worries about managing your children

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.650
who can't go to school, your distance, any?

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:37.650
It's unspeakable.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.650
And let me tell you why.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:39.650
In Europe, the reaction was so much better.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:40.930
The European leaders, whether their right
wing, left wing or in the middle, they know

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:41.930
the following.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.300
Labor unions, socialist parties, communist
parties, they're much stronger in Europe than

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.450
they are in the United States.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:46.702
And that's been true for a long time.

00:04:46.702 --> 00:04:49.740
And everyone there knows if you had dared
throw tens of millions of working people out

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.320
of their jobs, out of the security of their
jobs, they wouldn't have gone home to watch

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.320
TV.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.480
They would have gone into the streets and
they would have shut down those economies

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.050
way more totally than any pandemic could.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.050
And everyone there knew it.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.520
So here was the deal in Europe.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.230
The government will help you in your business,
but you cannot fire any one.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.630
You get it.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:09.630
That's the condition.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.750
And that was accepted in Europe and that's
how they've handled it here in the United

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.750
States.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.750
We don't have the political muscle on the
left in the labor movement to create a comparable

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:16.750
awareness.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:17.750
So the Trump administration.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:18.750
Good.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:19.750
Go ahead.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:20.750
The Democrats provided very little opposition.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.440
Even the AFL CIO hasn't had a demonstration
to say this is taking out the difficulties

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.000
of a virus on the mass of people who have
no guilt whatsoever.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.110
Why are they singled out?

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.550
Why is it possible to impose unemployment
on 50 million people while the richest people

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:32.550
gain billions?

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.550
This is beyond words.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.430
And my book is an attempt to try to get at
how this came about and how it works so that

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:37.430
people can contextualize these awful results.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.430
Yeah.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.430
The book is The Sickness Is the System.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:40.560
When capitalism fails to save us from pandemics
or itself.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.090
We're going to go to a break on the TV and
radio show with Professor Richard Wolf.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:47.550
But you'll be able to find our entire conversation,
which will continue on on the YouTube channel,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.500
The David Pakman Show David Pakman dot com.

